-- title:
--   Local rationality and functional equation at a bad place
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field of degree $3$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, with $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra structure on $\mathcal{O}_K$ integral, let $\psi$ be an additive character of the adele ring of $\mathbb{Q}$ that is trivial on principal adeles, continuous and nontrivial, and let $\mu$ be an admissible twist of $K$, i.e. a continuous homomorphism on the ideles of $K$ to $\mathbb{C}^\times$ trivial on principal ideles and of absolute value $1$. Assume: at every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ which is neither ramified in $K$ nor carries a ramified component of $\mu$ above it, the level `addCharLevel` of $\psi_v$ is $0$; and $\mu$ is not a norm twist, in the sense that no admissible twist $\eta$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ satisfies $\mu(\varpi_{\mathfrak{P}}) = \eta(\varpi_{\mathfrak{P}\cap\mathbb{Q}})^{f(\mathfrak{P})}$ for all $\mathfrak{P}$ at which both $\mu$ and $\eta$ are unramified. Let $D$, $U$, `gen` be carrier data on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles of $\mathbb{Q}$, let $S$ be a finite set of finite places which is exactly the set of bad places (ramified in $K$ or with $\mu$ ramified above), and let $X$ be cubic induction data (a form, a global and local and archimedean Whittaker function, a central character, a dual Whittaker function) satisfying `IsCubicInductionDataOn` for $K$, the production pins of $D$, $U$, `gen` and the adelic box, $\psi$, $\mu$ and $S$: the automorphy and central laws, cuspidality along the two maximal parabolics, the Whittaker expansion and Whittaker transformation laws, factorisation into local and archimedean factors outside $S$, sphericity and level invariance off $S$, Whittaker multiplicity one, moderate growth, $K$-finiteness, the iota-moment and half-plane conditions, and the dual-side counterparts (summarised here). Assume further that the form and both Whittaker functions are continuous, that the global and dual Whittaker functions are gauge-majorised, that $c$ is a complex number inverse to the adelic Haar volume of the adelic box, that the form is nonzero and that at every good place of level $0$ the local Whittaker function takes the value $1$ at the identity and has the spherical torus values attached to the induced coefficients of $\mu$, that at the bad places in any finite set the local Whittaker function is invariant under some open subgroup on the right and its cyclic $\mathrm{GL}_3$-span is generated by each of its nonzero members, that $E$ splits the archimedean ideles into the ideles (infinite part $u$, finite part $1$), and that a Haar measure $\nu_{\mathrm{mul}}$ is fixed on the archimedean idele units. Fix $v \in S$ and assume: the local Whittaker function takes the value $1$ at the identity at all $w \in S$ with $w \neq v$; at each $w \in S$ the right-invariant vectors of the cyclic span of the local Whittaker function under any open subgroup lie in the span of a finite set depending only on that subgroup; at each $w \in S$ the local component of the central character is unitary and the local Whittaker function transforms by it under the scalar torus; and the $S$-part integrands of the $\mathrm{GL}_3 \times \mathrm{GL}_1$ unfolding and of its dual are integrable in a right half-plane, for every admissible twist of $\mathbb{Q}$ and every adelic point. Then for every homomorphism $\chi$ from $(\mathbb{Q}_v)^\times$ to $\mathbb{C}^\times$ that has a conductor exponent, and for which there exist $g_\infty \in \mathrm{GL}_3$ of the infinite adeles and an admissible twist $\sigma$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ with archimedean type $(t,e)$ at every real place and $\chi(-1) = (-1)^e$ such that the archimedean zeta integral `archZeta30` of the translated archimedean Whittaker function against $\sigma \circ E$ is nonzero at some point of every right half-plane, there exist polynomials $R_1, R_2 \in \mathbb{C}[X]$, both nonzero, and $m \in \mathbb{Z}$, such that for every $g \in \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_v)$ there are polynomials $Q_1, Q_2$ with $Q_2 \neq 0$, an integer $n$ and reals $\sigma_0, \sigma_1$ with: the local zeta integral `localZeta30` of the local Whittaker function against $\chi$ at $g$, taken with respect to the multiplicative measure induced from the self-dual Haar measure at $v$, converges absolutely for $\mathrm{Re}\,s > \sigma_0$ and satisfies there $Z(s,g)\,Q_2(q_v^{-s}) = Q_1(q_v^{-s})\,q_v^{ns}$, where $q_v$ is the absolute norm of $v$; and the dual integral is absolutely convergent for the dual Whittaker function against $\chi^{-1}$ at $w' \cdot {}^{t}g^{-1}$ in the region $\mathrm{Re}(1-s) > \sigma_1$, where it satisfies $\widetilde{Z}(1-s,g)\,Q_2(q_v^{-s})R_2(q_v^{-s}) = R_1(q_v^{-s})Q_1(q_v^{-s})\,q_v^{(m+n)s}$.
--
--   This is the local statement at a ramified place extracted from the global $\mathrm{GL}_3 \times \mathrm{GL}_1$ functional equation in the Jacquet–Piatetski-Shapiro–Shalika theory: the local zeta integral at $v$ is, up to a power of $q_v$, a rational function of $q_v^{-s}$, and its dual satisfies a functional equation with a factor $R_1/R_2$ independent of the point $g$. It is used downstream to analyse the dual zeta integrals on the cyclic span of the local Whittaker function at deep bad places and to obtain rationality of the Euler factor at places ramified in $K$.
